1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,700
A hundred years ago, a scratch
from a rose bush could kill

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:04,510
you.

3
00:00:04,667 --> 00:00:07,397
So could a sore throat, a cut
while shaving,

4
00:00:07,407 --> 00:00:08,567
or giving birth.

5
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:12,683
In nineteen twenty four, the
sixteen year old son

6
00:00:12,693 --> 00:00:16,043
of the American president got a
blister playing tennis at

7
00:00:16,053 --> 00:00:16,853
the White House.

8
00:00:17,213 --> 00:00:19,793
A week later, he was dead from
blood poisoning.

9
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:23,513
Doctors had nothing that could
stop a bacterial infection

10
00:00:23,523 --> 00:00:25,263
once it spread through the body.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,080
Then, in a messy London
laboratory,

12
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:31,050
a scientist came back from his
holiday,

13
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:34,430
and noticed something odd on a
dish he had forgotten

14
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:35,240
to clean.

15
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:37,760
That accident gave the world
penicillin,

16
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:39,250
the first true antibiotic.

17
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:42,630
It has saved many millions of
lives.

18
00:00:43,583 --> 00:00:46,753
But the story of how it got from
that dish into hospitals is far

19
00:00:46,763 --> 00:00:47,293
stranger,

20
00:00:47,303 --> 00:00:50,323
and took far longer, than most
people know.

21
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:55,270
Alexander Fleming was born on a
farm in Scotland in

22
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,556
eighteen eighty one.

23
00:00:57,083 --> 00:01:00,383
He moved to London, trained as a
doctor at St Mary's Hospital,

24
00:01:00,803 --> 00:01:02,593
and stayed there for the rest of
his career,

25
00:01:02,603 --> 00:01:03,683
studying bacteria.

26
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:08,180
In the First World War, he
worked in battlefield hospitals
in

27
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:08,500
France,

28
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:11,170
and watched soldiers die of
infected wounds.

29
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:15,193
The antiseptics of the day often
did more harm

30
00:01:15,203 --> 00:01:16,003
than good.

31
00:01:16,303 --> 00:01:18,123
They killed the body's own
defences,

32
00:01:18,743 --> 00:01:19,903
along with the germs.

33
00:01:20,583 --> 00:01:22,883
Fleming spent years searching
for something better.

34
00:01:23,583 --> 00:01:26,623
In nineteen twenty two, he
discovered lysozyme,

35
00:01:27,043 --> 00:01:30,303
a natural germ killer found in
tears and mucus.

36
00:01:30,683 --> 00:01:32,883
But it was too weak to stop
serious infections.

37
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,180
He was a brilliant scientist,

38
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:37,870
but famously untidy.

39
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,880
His bench was always covered in
old culture plates.

40
00:01:41,833 --> 00:01:43,903
He even made paintings out of
bacteria,

41
00:01:43,913 --> 00:01:47,093
using germs that grew in
different colours on the dish.

42
00:01:48,733 --> 00:01:51,023
In the summer of nineteen twenty
eight,

43
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:54,403
Fleming left a stack of dishes
growing Staphylococcus

44
00:01:54,413 --> 00:01:56,853
bacteria on his bench, and went
on holiday.

45
00:01:57,667 --> 00:02:00,027
When he came back, on the third
of September,

46
00:02:00,447 --> 00:02:01,927
he started sorting through them.

47
00:02:02,583 --> 00:02:06,020
One dish was contaminated with a
fluffy blue-green mould.

48
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,960
Mould on a culture plate was
nothing unusual.

49
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:12,340
But around this mould, the
bacteria had vanished.

50
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:15,410
Something leaking out of the
mould was killing them.

51
00:02:16,083 --> 00:02:19,173
The mould spores had probably
drifted up from a laboratory

52
00:02:19,183 --> 00:02:22,313
on the floor below, where
another scientist was

53
00:02:22,323 --> 00:02:23,123
studying fungi.

54
00:02:23,723 --> 00:02:26,553
And a spell of cool weather let
the mould grow before the

55
00:02:26,563 --> 00:02:27,443
bacteria did.

56
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:31,347
Fleming is often quoted as
saying: that's funny.

57
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:34,157
The mould was a species of
Penicillium,

58
00:02:34,477 --> 00:02:37,437
a relative of the mould you
might find on old bread.

59
00:02:37,977 --> 00:02:40,437
So he called the mystery
substance penicillin.

60
00:02:41,167 --> 00:02:44,397
He found it could kill many of
the bacteria that cause disease,

61
00:02:44,407 --> 00:02:47,187
and that it seemed harmless to
white blood cells.

62
00:02:48,167 --> 00:02:50,895
He published his results in
nineteen twenty nine.

63
00:02:52,317 --> 00:02:54,157
And then, almost nothing
happened.

64
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,260
Penicillin was fragile.

65
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:00,190
It broke down quickly, and
Fleming's team could not purify

66
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:00,570
it,

67
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,360
or make more than tiny amounts.

68
00:03:03,333 --> 00:03:06,203
Fleming mostly used it as a tool
in his lab,

69
00:03:06,213 --> 00:03:08,773
to sort one kind of bacteria
from another.

70
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,890
For about ten years, one of the
most important discoveries

71
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:14,980
in medical history sat on a
shelf.

72
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:19,870
In nineteen thirty nine, at the
University of Oxford,

73
00:03:20,390 --> 00:03:23,860
an Australian scientist named
Howard Florey and a German-born

74
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:27,060
chemist named Ernst Chain dug up
Fleming's old

75
00:03:27,070 --> 00:03:27,870
paper.

76
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:31,720
Chain, a brilliant biochemist,
worked out how to

77
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:33,710
extract and concentrate the
drug.

78
00:03:34,417 --> 00:03:37,347
But they needed huge amounts of
mould juice to make even a

79
00:03:37,357 --> 00:03:38,197
little penicillin.

80
00:03:38,657 --> 00:03:40,037
And Britain was at war.

81
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,330
So their colleague Norman
Heatley improvised.

82
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:46,100
He grew the mould in anything he
could find:

83
00:03:46,110 --> 00:03:49,610
bedpans, milk churns, baths, and
food tins.

84
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:53,350
He even designed special ceramic
culture vessels,

85
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:55,460
and the team hired young women,

86
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:58,790
nicknamed the penicillin girls,
to tend them.

87
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:02,300
On the twenty fifth of May,
nineteen forty,

88
00:04:02,310 --> 00:04:03,770
they ran a decisive test.

89
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:07,700
Eight mice were injected with
deadly streptococcus

90
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:08,510
bacteria.

91
00:04:09,083 --> 00:04:11,343
Four of them were also given
penicillin.

92
00:04:11,783 --> 00:04:12,923
The other four were not.

93
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:16,830
By the next morning, all four
untreated mice were dead.

94
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:18,970
All four treated mice were
alive.

95
00:04:19,750 --> 00:04:21,690
Chain reportedly danced with
excitement.

96
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:23,910
Florey said it looked like a
miracle.

97
00:04:24,833 --> 00:04:27,863
They published the results in
August nineteen forty.

98
00:04:27,873 --> 00:04:30,303
And they were so worried about a
German invasion that they

99
00:04:30,313 --> 00:04:32,803
rubbed mould spores into the
linings of their coats,

100
00:04:32,813 --> 00:04:34,553
so the work could survive.

101
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:37,033
Next came people.

102
00:04:37,453 --> 00:04:40,803
In February nineteen forty one,
a forty three year old

103
00:04:40,813 --> 00:04:44,223
policeman named Albert Alexander
lay dying in an Oxford

104
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:45,033
hospital.

105
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:49,220
A small scratch near his mouth,
often said to be from a

106
00:04:49,230 --> 00:04:52,460
rose thorn, had become a raging
infection that spread

107
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:54,530
to his eyes, face and lungs.

108
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:57,790
He was given penicillin, and
within a day,

109
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:58,880
he began to improve.

110
00:04:59,833 --> 00:05:00,933
But there wasn't enough.

111
00:05:01,393 --> 00:05:04,623
The team even recovered
penicillin from his urine to use

112
00:05:04,633 --> 00:05:05,433
again.

113
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:08,170
After five days, the supply ran
out.

114
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:12,080
The infection came back, and
Albert Alexander died

115
00:05:12,090 --> 00:05:12,890
in March.

116
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:14,300
It was heartbreaking.

117
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:16,660
But it proved the drug worked in
humans.

118
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:18,960
The problem was making enough of
it.

119
00:05:19,583 --> 00:05:21,223
A year later, in New Haven,

120
00:05:22,043 --> 00:05:22,693
Connecticut,

121
00:05:22,703 --> 00:05:25,833
a woman named Anne Miller was
dying of blood poisoning

122
00:05:25,843 --> 00:05:26,943
after a miscarriage.

123
00:05:27,803 --> 00:05:28,883
Penicillin saved her.

124
00:05:29,583 --> 00:05:32,593
Her treatment used about half of
all the penicillin in the

125
00:05:32,603 --> 00:05:33,843
United States at the time.

126
00:05:34,223 --> 00:05:35,983
She lived to the age of ninety.

127
00:05:36,917 --> 00:05:40,267
Later that year, after a
terrible nightclub fire

128
00:05:40,277 --> 00:05:41,077
in Boston,

129
00:05:41,357 --> 00:05:43,577
penicillin was used to treat
burn victims,

130
00:05:44,157 --> 00:05:46,857
and the public began to hear
about the new miracle drug.

131
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:51,973
To make penicillin in bulk,
Florey and Heatley flew to the

132
00:05:51,983 --> 00:05:55,453
United States in nineteen forty
one, carrying samples

133
00:05:55,463 --> 00:05:56,263
of the mould.

134
00:05:56,917 --> 00:05:59,657
At a government lab in Peoria,
Illinois,

135
00:06:00,137 --> 00:06:02,687
scientists grew the mould in
huge tanks,

136
00:06:02,697 --> 00:06:05,987
fed with corn steep liquor, a
cheap leftover

137
00:06:05,997 --> 00:06:07,617
from making corn starch.

138
00:06:08,417 --> 00:06:09,217
Yields soared.

139
00:06:09,437 --> 00:06:12,507
Then a lab worker named Mary
Hunt brought in a mouldy

140
00:06:12,517 --> 00:06:14,537
cantaloupe from a local market.

141
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:18,723
Its mould produced far more
penicillin than Fleming's
original

142
00:06:18,733 --> 00:06:19,533
strain.

143
00:06:19,693 --> 00:06:23,123
Most penicillin made since then
comes from descendants of

144
00:06:23,133 --> 00:06:23,993
that melon mould.

145
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:27,150
American drug companies scaled
up fast.

146
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:31,340
Production rose from almost
nothing in early nineteen forty
two

147
00:06:31,350 --> 00:06:33,010
to millions of doses.

148
00:06:33,917 --> 00:06:36,707
By the time Allied troops landed
in Normandy on

149
00:06:36,717 --> 00:06:37,627
D-Day,

150
00:06:37,637 --> 00:06:40,927
in June nineteen forty four,
there was enough penicillin

151
00:06:40,937 --> 00:06:41,817
to treat the wounded.

152
00:06:42,583 --> 00:06:44,613
Soldiers called it the miracle
drug.

153
00:06:44,623 --> 00:06:47,233
Infected wounds that would once
have meant

154
00:06:47,243 --> 00:06:49,763
amputation or death could now
heal.

155
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,660
So how does penicillin actually
kill bacteria?

156
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:57,810
Most bacteria are wrapped in a
tough cell wall,

157
00:06:58,230 --> 00:07:01,230
which they constantly rebuild as
they grow and divide.

158
00:07:02,083 --> 00:07:04,963
Penicillin jams the machinery
that builds that wall.

159
00:07:05,667 --> 00:07:09,097
The wall weakens, water rushes
in, and the bacterium

160
00:07:09,107 --> 00:07:09,357
bursts,

161
00:07:09,367 --> 00:07:12,837
while our own cells, which have
no such wall,

162
00:07:12,847 --> 00:07:14,007
are left alone.

163
00:07:14,833 --> 00:07:17,850
In nineteen forty five, Dorothy
Hodgkin used

164
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:20,903
X-ray crystallography to reveal
penicillin's

165
00:07:20,913 --> 00:07:21,713
structure,

166
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:24,113
including its unusual four-sided
ring.

167
00:07:25,650 --> 00:07:28,820
That same year, Fleming, Florey
and Chain shared the Nobel Prize

168
00:07:28,830 --> 00:07:30,470
in Physiology or Medicine.

169
00:07:31,333 --> 00:07:34,383
Norman Heatley, whose improvised
bedpans made it all

170
00:07:34,393 --> 00:07:35,193
possible,

171
00:07:35,333 --> 00:07:36,133
was left out.

172
00:07:36,613 --> 00:07:37,473
Decades later,

173
00:07:38,093 --> 00:07:41,363
Oxford gave him its first
honorary doctorate in medicine
in its

174
00:07:41,373 --> 00:07:42,593
eight hundred year history.

175
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:46,723
Fleming became world famous, and
he always insisted that

176
00:07:46,733 --> 00:07:48,433
luck had played a big part.

177
00:07:49,167 --> 00:07:51,997
Fleming was knighted in nineteen
forty four.

178
00:07:52,007 --> 00:07:55,357
When he died in nineteen fifty
five, he was buried in

179
00:07:55,367 --> 00:07:57,207
St Paul's Cathedral in London.

180
00:07:57,917 --> 00:08:00,157
And in his Nobel lecture, he
gave a warning.

181
00:08:00,833 --> 00:08:04,023
If people took too little
penicillin, or stopped too

182
00:08:04,033 --> 00:08:04,833
soon,

183
00:08:04,893 --> 00:08:06,733
bacteria could learn to resist
it.

184
00:08:08,233 --> 00:08:09,033
He was right.

185
00:08:09,313 --> 00:08:10,373
Bacteria evolve.

186
00:08:10,733 --> 00:08:14,223
Each time we use an antibiotic,
the few that happen to

187
00:08:14,233 --> 00:08:15,413
survive can multiply.

188
00:08:16,333 --> 00:08:19,563
Today, drug-resistant infections
are one of the biggest

189
00:08:19,573 --> 00:08:21,013
threats to global health.

190
00:08:21,573 --> 00:08:25,038
In twenty nineteen, they
directly caused an estimated one

191
00:08:25,048 --> 00:08:27,353
point two seven million deaths.

192
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:31,720
That's why doctors ask us to
take antibiotics only when

193
00:08:31,730 --> 00:08:34,330
we need them, and exactly as
prescribed.

194
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,450
Penicillin and the antibiotics
that followed it changed
medicine

195
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:39,260
completely.

196
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:42,430
They made surgery, cancer
treatment and organ

197
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:43,920
transplants far safer.

198
00:08:44,750 --> 00:08:47,130
Penicillin also started a gold
rush.

199
00:08:47,990 --> 00:08:51,110
Within a few years scientists
found streptomycin,

200
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:54,180
the first drug that could cure
tuberculosis,

201
00:08:54,190 --> 00:08:56,650
and dozens more antibiotics
followed.

202
00:08:58,233 --> 00:09:01,323
It all began with a dirty dish,
and a scientist curious

203
00:09:01,333 --> 00:09:02,593
enough to look twice.

204
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:06,950
As Fleming later said: one
sometimes finds what one

205
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:07,960
is not looking for.
